{"parentUuid":"05f3344d-f3a9-44a8-b8d6-49598459ea74","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1ec9ed76-4982-4973-85e2-3f6d3abc155e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0168wDUUreu9w974n7vCUaGW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// Package types defines the per-agent abstraction interfaces for `entire review`.\n2\t//\n3\t// AgentReviewer is the contract every supported agent (claude-code, codex,\n4\t// gemini-cli, future additions) implements in its own package. The orchestrator\n5\t// in cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go consumes this interface, never importing\n6\t// concrete agent packages — that's how new agents land as additive files\n7\t// without touching shared code.\n8\t//\n9\t// Events flow as a stream: implementations spawn the agent process, parse\n10\t// stdout into a sequence of typed Events (Started, AssistantText, ToolCall,\n11\t// Tokens, Finished, RunError), and surface them via Process.Events. Per-agent\n12\t// quirks (codex's chrome stripping, gemini's stdin requirement, claude's argv\n13\t// shape) are entirely encapsulated inside each agent's adapter — shared code\n14\t// only sees the cleaned event stream.\n15\t//\n16\t// Living in a subpackage (not the review root package) avoids import cycles:\n17\t// per-agent reviewers and the orchestrator both depend on these types\n18\t// without depending on each other.\n19\tpackage types\n20\t\n21\timport \"context\"\n22\t\n23\t// AgentReviewer drives a single agent's review run.\n24\ttype AgentReviewer interface {\n25\t\t// Name returns the agent's registry key (e.g., \"claude-code\", \"codex\",\n26\t\t// \"gemini-cli\"). Stable identifier; do not change after release without\n27\t\t// updating settings migration.\n28\t\tName() string\n29\t\n30\t\t// Start spawns the agent with the given run configuration. The returned\n31\t\t// Process exposes streaming events via Events() and a Wait() that returns\n32\t\t// when the process exits.\n33\t\t//\n34\t\t// Implementations MUST set the ENTIRE_REVIEW_* env vars on the spawned\n35\t\t// child process (see cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go) so the agent's\n36\t\t// lifecycle hooks adopt the session as a review session.\n37\t\t//\n38\t\t// Errors from Start indicate failure to construct or launch the process\n39\t\t// (e.g., binary not on PATH at exec.Cmd.Start time, invalid argv). Once\n40\t\t// Start returns nil, errors during the run flow through Process.Events\n41\t\t// (as RunError) and Process.Wait.\n42\t\tStart(ctx context.Context, run RunConfig) (Process, error)\n43\t}\n44\t\n45\t// Process represents a running agent review.\n46\ttype Process interface {\n47\t\t// Events returns a channel that emits structured events as the agent\n48\t\t// produces output. The channel is closed when the process exits — cleanly,\n49\t\t// with error, or via context cancellation. Consumers should range over it.\n50\t\tEvents() <-chan Event\n51\t\n52\t\t// Wait blocks until the process exits and returns:\n53\t\t//   - nil on clean exit (exit code 0)\n54\t\t//   - ctx.Err() on cancellation\n55\t\t//   - an error wrapping *exec.ExitError on non-zero exit\n56\t\t//   - other error types for I/O or pipe failures\n57\t\t//\n58\t\t// Wait must be called exactly once per Process. It is safe to call Wait\n59\t\t// after the Events channel has closed. Consumers must drain Events until\n60\t\t// close before calling Wait; otherwise an implementation that forwards\n61\t\t// parsed events from another goroutine may block while sending.\n62\t\tWait() error\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t// RunConfig is the immutable per-run configuration passed to AgentReviewer.Start.\n66\t//\n67\t// All fields are optional from a marshalling standpoint (zero value is valid),\n68\t// but Skills is typically non-empty in practice — it carries the skill\n69\t// invocations (e.g., \"/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\") the configured agent\n70\t// should run.\n71\ttype RunConfig struct {\n72\t\t// PromptOverride, when non-empty, is the exact prompt sent to the agent.\n73\t\t// It preserves settings.ReviewConfig.Prompt's existing verbatim-override\n74\t\t// contract: configured skills are still recorded as structured metadata,\n75\t\t// but they are not prepended to the prompt text.\n76\t\tPromptOverride string\n77\t\n78\t\t// Skills are skill invocation strings passed to the agent verbatim.\n79\t\tSkills []string\n80\t\n81\t\t// AlwaysPrompt is the per-agent always-prompt configured in settings.\n82\t\t// Concatenated with Skills + PerRunPrompt + a scope clause to form the\n83\t\t// composed agent prompt.\n84\t\tAlwaysPrompt string\n85\t\n86\t\t// PerRunPrompt is optional textarea input from a single invocation.\n87\t\tPerRunPrompt string\n88\t\n89\t\t// ScopeBaseRef is the git ref the review is scoped against (typically the\n90\t\t// closest ancestor branch). Used to compose the scope clause and as the\n91\t\t// base for `git diff` operations the agent may perform.\n92\t\tScopeBaseRef string\n93\t\n94\t\t// CheckpointContext is best-effort context derived from checkpoints in the\n95\t\t// branch review scope. It is appended to generated prompts so every agent\n96\t\t// can use checkpoint IDs, file summaries, and transcript lookup commands\n97\t\t// while reviewing. PromptOverride remains verbatim and does not receive\n98\t\t// this context.\n99\t\tCheckpointContext string\n100\t\n101\t\t// StartingSHA is HEAD at invocation time, propagated to the lifecycle\n102\t\t// hook via ENTIRE_REVIEW_STARTING_SHA so checkpoint metadata records\n103\t\t// the commit that was reviewed.\n104\t\tStartingSHA string\n105\t}\n106\t\n107\t// Event is the sealed sum type emitted by Process.Events. The unexported\n108\t// isEvent method prevents external packages from adding event variants;\n109\t// adding a new event requires updating this file (and every consumer's\n110\t// type switch — that's intentional, since a new event variant is a contract\n111\t// change).\n112\ttype Event interface{ isEvent() }\n113\t\n114\t// Started signals the agent