{"parentUuid":"20975d44-b389-4596-b10e-da09ed1da114","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01L9nbHepFcEfMg8bwjHEmod","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015rRpzKe5yyiU5A5MfbYCkD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/review-fix-progress-stream/e2e/streaming-smoke/main.go","content":"// streaming-smoke is a self-contained verifier for the per-agent live\n// streaming fix in `entire review`. It does NOT call any agent API.\n//\n// What it proves\n//\n// The fix routes each agent's stdout through a pseudo-terminal so the\n// child's isatty(stdout) returns true and its runtime (libc / Rust / Node)\n// reverts to line-buffered flushing instead of block-buffering until exit.\n// To exercise that mechanism without burning tokens, this binary spawns\n// ITSELF as a child in \"probe\" mode — and the probe deliberately\n// block-buffers stdout when stdout is a pipe. So:\n//\n//   - With UsePTY=false: the probe sees a pipe, fills its 64KB buffer,\n//     and only flushes at exit. The runner observes one big burst.\n//   - With UsePTY=true:  the probe sees a TTY, line-buffers, and the\n//     runner observes events arriving as each line is written.\n//\n// The two passes print event timestamps side-by-side so the difference\n// is visible at a glance.\n//\n// Usage:\n//\n//\tgo run ./e2e/streaming-smoke\n//\n// Expected output (timestamps approximate):\n//\n//\t=== Pass 1: UsePTY=false (simulates the bug) ===\n//\t  +    0ms  Started{}\n//\t  +  830ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 1\"\n//\t  +  830ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 2\"\n//\t  +  830ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 3\"\n//\t  +  830ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 4\"\n//\t  +  830ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 5\"\n//\t  +  830ms  Finished{Success:true}\n//\n//\t=== Pass 2: UsePTY=true (the fix) ===\n//\t  +    0ms  Started{}\n//\t  +    1ms  AssistantText  \"probe line 1\"\n//\t 