{"parentUuid":"80f7fd2c-35ba-43af-8ed8-0ed5c578d6d7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QiT1HqMteBAxnkVTeuwWKc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cleanup done. Final state:\n\n```\n M .entire/settings.json                              (your local review config — keep or unstage)\n M cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/reviewer_test.go   (+TestParseClaudeOutput_StreamsPerLine)\n M cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template_test.go       (+skipOnWindows helper)\n```\n\n★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\nThe whole PTY arc was an instructive false start. The brief's \"events buffered until exit\" framing read like a runtime-level (libc/Node/Rust isatty) buffering bug, which would have made PTY the textbook fix. Empirical evidence — first the streaming parser test, then your `claude -p` and `codex exec` shell runs, then the real `entire review` runs — showed the parsers and the agents already stream over a pipe. The \"burst at end\" observation in the original brief was either inaccurate or about something else (TUI repaint cadence, perhaps). What we kept is exactly the artifact that would have caught this misdiagnosis sooner if it had existed: a test that pins the parser's line-by-line behaviour. Worth the detour.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n## What's left in this PR\n\n**`cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/reviewer_test.go`** — adds `TestParseClaudeOutput_StreamsPerLine`. Drips lines through `io.Pipe` and asserts each event arrives before the next line is written. Regression guard against any future \"parser silently stops being line-by-line\" change.\n\n**`cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template_test.go`** — adds a tiny `skipOnWindows(t, reason)` helper used by the two existing POSIX-shell tests. Equivalent to what was inline before, just deduped. Drops nothing.\n\n## What's NOT in this PR (and was)\n\n- The `UsePTY` field on `ReviewerTemplate` and its `pty.Open` / `ptyReader